OVERALL CONTEXT. The page belongs to Riemann's work on theta functions with half-integer characteristics and on the plane quartic (genus 3) written in the form sqrt(x*xi)+sqrt(y*eta)+sqrt(z*zeta)=0, where x,y,z,xi,eta,zeta are linear forms, i.e. bitangents. This is the material Weber later edited as 'Zur Theorie der Abel'schen Functionen fuer den Fall p=3' (Werke, 1876). The previous page has the same square-root identities. The next page has theta products theta(x+y+z+t)... and ratios (x/y)_{z=0} of linear forms at contact points. The page mixes five strands.

(A) SQUARE-ROOT FORM OF THE QUARTIC (top and upper right). The garbled top line is an attempt to write the quartic as a sum of three square roots of products of linear forms. On the right Riemann takes the relation as given: sqrt(x*xi)+sqrt(y*eta)+sqrt(z*zeta)=0.
- He squares it once in the form sqrt(x xi)+sqrt(y eta) = -sqrt(z zeta).
- This gives x xi + y eta - z zeta = -2 sqrt(x xi y eta). The machine transcription garbles this line.
- He then considers the further forms x+y+z and xi+eta+zeta. He writes identities of the type sqrt(x+y+z)*sqrt(xi+eta+zeta) = sqrt(x eta) -/+ sqrt(y xi), and two companions with permuted letters.
- The claim is that the sum forms are again bitangents, giving new representations of the same quartic. This is the standard Aronhold-system bookkeeping: x, y, z, x+y+z, ... and the conjugate system.
- These identities do NOT follow from the quartic equation alone. They require the specific linear normalisations among the six forms, which are not written here (my remark).

(B) QUADRATIC RELATIONS BETWEEN TWO TETRADS (left column). Riemann takes two quadruples of linear forms (x,y,z,t) and (x1,y1,z1,t1), normalised by x+y+z = x1+y1+z1 = t. He posits product relations:
- x1 y1 = xy+zt and z1 t1 = alpha xy + beta zt;
- x1 z1 = xz+yt and y1 t1 = gamma xz + delta yt;
- x1 t1 = xt+yz and y1 z1 = epsilon xt + zeta yz;
- square relations x1^2 = b y^2 + c z^2 + d t^2 and y1^2 = a' x^2 + c' z^2 + d' t^2, with z1^2 and t1^2 left blank.
This is exactly the shape of the classical quadratic relations among theta functions: squares of one set are linear in squares of another, and products xy+zt are of addition-theorem type, as for Goepel/Rosenhain tetrads.
- In the centre he forms three combinations p, q, r of differences of squares (x^2-t^2, z^2-t^2, y^2-t^2, t^2-x^2, t^2-y^2). The coefficients are built from constants g, h, a, b, c, d.
- Beside each he writes a product of two such coefficients, e.g. g(a-hd)(gd+hc).
- He then forms p*g(a-hd)/(hd+gc). The evident aim is to eliminate t^2 and obtain one linear relation among p, q, r, i.e. a consistency condition on the constants.
- The computation is not completed on the page.

(C) CHARACTERISTIC TABLES (middle). Genus-3 half-period characteristics are written as 2x3 arrays of 0/1. Six basis characteristics a, b, c, d, e, f are fixed explicitly, in several different choices on the page. Every characteristic is then expressed as a sum of these, in rows such as:
- o, b, c / a, a+b, a+c;
- d, d+a+b, d+a+c, d+a+b+c;
- e+b, e+a+b, ...; f+b, f+a+b, ....
Columns of four 3-bit rows list the resulting characteristics. These are grouped into cases (b), (c), (d) with annotations '+b+c' and '=2'.
This is enumeration of systems of characteristics: which sums are even or odd, and which groups of four or eight close up. It is the combinatorics behind the 28 bitangents and the Goepel-type groups used in the theta relations of (B).
At the bottom a 4-bit version appears: o, b, c, b+c / a, a+b, a+c, a+b+c, with 2x4 arrays. This is the analogous enumeration for one more variable, i.e. general p.

(D) RATIONAL FUNCTIONS / CORRESPONDENCES IN s, z. The glyph read as '5' by the transcriber is Riemann's s. Lines include:
- s-z and s-z+1;
- z^2 s z + s - z;
- z^2(s-a)(z-a)+s-z;
- s^2+alpha s+beta + (s^2+gamma s+delta) z;
- prescriptions 's=alpha, z=beta' and 's=alpha, z=infinity';
- (z-beta)(s+h)+(s+h)(s-alpha);
- an expansion with binomial coefficients in powers of s, and the monomial list 1, s, z, s^2, sz, z^2 with counts 2 3 4 3 2.
He is building (2,2)- and low-degree relations between s and z with prescribed corresponding point pairs. This is continued on the next page: asz+bs+cz+d, with conditions like b-c=2.
Alongside is pure factor bookkeeping with twelve labelled linear factors:
- p = 1·2·3·4·5·6 and q = 7·8·9·10·11·12;
- t = 7·8·9·10·5·6 and u = 1·2·3·4·11·12;
- hence pq = tu;
- q/u = t/p = (7·8·9·10)/(1·2·3·4);
- u/p = q/t = (11·12)/(5·6).

(E) CONSTANT- AND DEGREE-COUNTS FOR GENERAL p (bottom).
- '2^{4(p-2)} Grad der Gleichung zwischen x1 und x2' and '2^{2p-2} Grad der Gleichung zwischen x1^2 und x2^2'.
- 2^{p-1} written next to x1^{1/2} x2^{1/2} ... xp^{1/2}. This is the number of sign choices, and hence the degree in the square roots, when a relation among p square roots sqrt(x_i) is rationalised (my interpretation).
- '2p-3 Punkte, 2^{2p-5}' and '2(2p-3) Groessen, 4(p-2) Gleichungen, Groessen mehr'. Here 2(2p-3) - 4(p-2) = 2 more unknowns than equations.
- 'p-1+m, m Gleichungen'.
- '2(p-1+m) unknowns, p-2+2m Gleichungen', with excess p.
- Further lines: p-2+3m, 2p-2+2m, p+m = 2.
This is a parameter count testing whether the genus-3 square-root representation generalises to arbitrary p: whether a curve can be written by relations among square roots of linear or abelian forms, and how many free constants remain.

(F) DRAWING. Four pairs of overlapping circles joined in a chain by line segments. Arrows and crosses are on the first two pairs. This is a schematic of a genus-p surface (here 4 handles) with a canonical pair of cycles per handle, i.e. Riemann's system of cross-cuts.
